From 0770ade29ab93052002df1becd66672206dbd1c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 14:09:05 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [Channel Selection]: Add CBS based channel selection with 
 preference-table cache

Add CBS-rank-based channel selection: periodically trigger the QCA driver's Channel
Busy Scan (CBS), parse the resulting "ACS show_report", cache it as a per-radio channel
preference table, and on a Channel Selection Request pick the best (op-class, channel,
bandwidth) from the controller-preferred set by driver rank and perform the channel
switch. Everything is additive and gated behind a new, default-disabled config knob
(cbs_based_chansel_enable); when it is off the existing channel-selection and ZWDFS flows
are byte-for-byte unchanged.

Config (default OFF, mutually exclusive with Active-ACS):
- beerocks_agent.conf.in: add cbs_based_chansel_enable (0/1) and
  cbs_trigger_periodic_interval (seconds, 0..86400; 0 = disabled).
- beerocks_config_file.{h,cpp}: parse both keys into sConfigSlave.
- agent_db.h (sDeviceConf): add cbs_based_chansel_enable (bool) and
  cbs_trigger_periodic_interval (uint32).
- beerocks_agent_main.cpp: populate/validate the knobs and enforce mutual exclusion --
  if cbs_based_chansel_enable and active_acs_chansel_enable are both set, disable BOTH and
  fall back to the original (controller + radio preference) logic, with an error logged.

Periodic trigger transport (dedicated VS message, no flag round-trip through the HAL):
The config knobs live in the backhaul/son_slave process (that is where beerocks_agent.conf
is parsed into AgentDB), but the driver command must run in the separate ap_manager/HAL
process whose AgentDB copy is NOT populated with those knobs. A HAL-side flag check would
therefore always read false and never trigger. Instead the periodic decision is made where
the config is valid (ChannelSelectionTask) and shipped down as a dedicated Vendor-Specific
message, mirroring every other channel-selection action already in the codebase:
- beerocks_message_action.yaml: add ACTION_BACKHAUL_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS (91) and
  ACTION_APMANAGER_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS (92); bump the ENUM_END sentinels.
- beerocks_message_backhaul.yaml / beerocks_message_apmanager.yaml + generated .h/.cpp:
  add the two field-less classes cACTION_BACKHAUL_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS and
  cACTION_APMANAGER_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS (radio identified via the beerocks action-header
  radio_mac(), so no payload fields are needed).
- ChannelSelectionTask::work(): the periodic timer (cbs_trigger_periodic_interval) now
  sends cACTION_BACKHAUL_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS per radio (gated on
  enable_auto_chansel_handling && cbs_based_chansel_enable && !active_acs_chansel_enable
  && interval>0).
- son_slave_thread.cpp: forward ACTION_BACKHAUL_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS to the owning radio's
  ap_manager as ACTION_APMANAGER_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS.
- ap_manager.cpp: handle ACTION_APMANAGER_HOSTAP_TRIGGER_CBS by calling
  ap_wlan_hal->trigger_cbs() unconditionally (the enable decision was already made on the
  backhaul side). This backend-specific trigger is isolated behind ap_wlan_hal->trigger_cbs()
  so a future pWHM/whm backend only needs to implement that one method; the timer, gating,
  radio iteration and result-handling stay backend-agnostic.

bwl HAL:
- base_wlan_hal_types.h: add sAcsChannelRank { channel, center_chan, rank } to carry one
  parsed ACS-report row out of the HAL; add hal_conf_t.cbs_based_chansel_enable (informational).
- ap_wlan_hal.h / ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.{h,cpp}:
  - read_acs_ranks(): send "ACS show_report" on the base radio hostapd control socket
    (dedicated 32 KB reply buffer, since a 6GHz report overflows the shared 4096-byte
    wpa_ctrl buffer) and parse the report table into sAcsChannelRank entries (isolated
    parser: skips header/divider, stops at summary lines, extracts channel/center/rank
    per row). Parsed rows are logged for verification.
  - trigger_cbs(): send "CBS enable 1" on the base radio socket to run a one-shot CBS.
  - read_acs_report(): now calls read_acs_ranks() and merges the ranks into
    m_radio_info.channels_list[channel].bw_info_list[bw] (best/min positive rank kept),
    resolving the specific 6GHz 320MHz variant (320-1/320-2) from the reported center
    channel so the fronthaul-written slot matches the backhaul-read slot; the ranks then
    reach the backhaul via the existing CHANNELS_LIST_RESPONSE (sSupportedBandwidth.rank)
    with no new payload TLV.
  - refresh_radio_info(): seed the per-(channel,bw) baseline rank to 0 (was 1) so an
    unranked channel is not mistaken for "rank 1 = best"; non-CBS flows are unaffected
    (all channels share the same baseline).
  - Map "CBS-COMPLETED" and "DYNAMIC-ACS-EVENT-COMPLETED" driver events to the existing
    ACS_Completed event so the report is read on completion.
- base_wlan_hal_nl80211.{h,cpp}: add wpa_ctrl_send_msg_bufsize() (caller-sized temporary
  reply buffer) used by read_acs_ranks().

ap_manager on ACS/CBS completion:
- hal_event_handler() ACS_Completed/CSA_Finished: run refresh_radio_info() BEFORE
  read_acs_report() (refresh rebuilds channels_list and re-seeds ranks to the baseline, so
  it must run first or it would wipe the parsed ranks). On ACS_Completed, proactively build
  and push a CHANNELS_LIST_RESPONSE carrying the fresh ranks so the backhaul CBS cache is
  refreshed immediately on completion (a CHANNEL_SELECTION_REQUEST arriving right after CBS
  no longer races a stale rank-0 cache).

AgentDB (agent_db.h, per-radio cache):
- sRadio: add sPreferenceEntry { channel, operating_class, bandwidth, rank, preference,
  reason } and sPreferenceTable { by_opclass_channel, valid, last_cbs_complete,
  cbs_measurement_bw }, plus a per-radio cbs_preference_table.

ChannelSelectionTask (channel_selection_task.{h,cpp}):
- rank_to_preference(): rank<0 -> 0 (non-operable); rank==0 -> 1 (operable baseline);
  rank>=1 -> clamp(16 - rank, 1, 15).
- sChannelBwRank { channel, bandwidth, rank } and refresh_preference_table(radio_mac,
  entries): builds cbs_preference_table directly from the already-known (channel, bandwidth,
  rank) tuples (derives op-class via get_operating_class_by_channel; skips invalid combos
  quietly), also updates sChannelInfo.supported_bw_list[bw].rank, overlays non-operable
  states (unsupported/AWGN/DFS-unavailable -> preference 0), sets the cache validity bit on
  success, and keeps the previous (stale) cache on empty input. Logged one entry per line
  (a single multi-line log is truncated by the syslog transport).
- build_preferred_channel_set(): shared helper turning the controller Channel Preference
  map into the set of preferred channel numbers (op-classes with empty channel lists expand
  to all channels; preference==0 entries are excluded).
- select_best_channel_from_cbs(radio_mac, controller_preferences, out): rank-first selection
  over the CBS cache intersected with the controller-preferred set. Only entries that are
  operable (preference>0) AND preferred are considered; the best (lowest positive) driver
  rank wins, tie-broken by wider bandwidth; if no preferred channel carries a positive rank
  it returns false so the caller falls back to the legacy cumulative-score selection. The
  winner is resolved to a beacon/primary channel and then upgraded to the radio's operating
  (widest supported) bandwidth. All candidates and the final selection are logged.
- handle_channel_selection_request(): common pre-selection rules (Rule 1: single preferred
  channel -> direct switch at current bw; Rule 2a: current channel already preferred -> no
  switch; Rule 2b: current not preferred -> approach-specific selection). When CBS is enabled
  (and Active-ACS is not), use select_best_channel_from_cbs() to populate the outgoing request
  and still build the block-list (all - preferred) for DCS safety; the pending-outgoing loop
  performs a real send_channel_switch_request() for the CBS case (it explicitly does NOT call
  send_acs_list_to_platform(), which would run "acs invoke 0" and override the chosen channel).
  Falls back to the legacy cumulative-score selection when CBS selection is unavailable.
- handle_vs_channels_list_response(): when CBS is enabled, rebuild the rank list from the
  refreshed channels_list and call refresh_preference_table() (self-gated no-op otherwise),
  with a diagnostic counting how many (chan,bw) entries carry a real positive driver rank.

No functional behavior change when cbs_based_chansel_enable is disabled (the default).

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
